WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.720
Pycelle: Without question, Oldtown is the
oldest, wealthiest, and greatest city in Westeros.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:11.570
Qyburn: Though King's Landing is more populous,
and arguably, more powerful.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:17.220
Pycelle: The city straddles the mouth of the
Honeywine River, and the bounty of the entire

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.350
Reach flows beneath its stone bridges to ports
that are renowned throughout the world.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.822
Qyburn: Thus, we needn't speak more of them.

00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:29.940
More interesting is the history of the city,
for none can say when it was founded.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.090
Many centuries ago, a few bold maesters sought
out the Children of the Forest, who claimed

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:39.190
that men have lived at the mouth of the Honeywine
since the Dawn Age.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:40.810
Pycelle: Nonsense.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:46.180
Maester Jellicoe proved that the city began
as a trading post where ships from Valyria,

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.100
Old Ghis, and the Summer Isles put in to replenish
their provisions and make repairs.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:57.480
Hence, the most famous feature of the city,
the Hightower, raised even before the city

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.120
walls as a beacon through the fog-covered
waters.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.890
Originally built of wood and standing a mere
fifty feet above the ancient fortress of its

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:13.469
foundation, it is now thick stone and rises
even taller than the Wall.

00:01:13.469 --> 00:01:17.969
Those born and raised in Oldtown can tell
the time of day by where its shadow falls.

00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:20.700
Qyburn: But no maester can tell why.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.959
Even in our oldest records, the island on
which it stands is called Battle Isle.

00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:29.189
What battle was fought there?

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:30.430
And when?

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:35.289
As for the ancient fortress that forms the
tower's base, its walls and interiors are

00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:38.679
all of solid black stone with no hints of
joints.

00:01:38.679 --> 00:01:39.780
Or mortar.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.380
And no chisel marks of any kind.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.969
We know it predates the tower itself by thousands
of years.

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:48.099
But who built it?

00:01:48.099 --> 00:01:49.099
How?

00:01:49.099 --> 00:01:50.439
And against what?

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:56.679
Pycelle: The maesters who have studied it
declare it to be of Valyrian construction,

00:01:56.679 --> 00:02:00.390
akin to Dragonstone and the Black Walls of
Volantis.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:05.810
As is well-known, the dragonlords of Valyria
could turn stone to liquid with dragonflame

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.000
to shape it as they wished.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.400
Qyburn: Didn't one Archmaester link the fortress
to the vanished mazemakers of Lorath?

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.190
And another maester to the legendary Deep
Ones who inhabited the Iron Islands before

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.660
the Ironborn?

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.910
Pycelle: Both marginal opinions without common
support.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:30.239
But of more importance than the origin of
the tower is the family to which it gave its

00:02:30.239 --> 00:02:31.239
name.

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:37.540
House Hightower.The ancient ruling family
of Oldtown.Known for their prudence and love

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:38.540
of peace.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.410
Qyburn: And pieces of gold.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:45.489
Pycelle: During the Age of Heroes, when Lymond
Hightower saw the Gardener kings conquering

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:50.549
the Reach, he didn't beat them on the battlefield,
but in the godswood.

00:02:50.549 --> 00:02:53.450
He bound his house to theirs by marriage.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:59.340
And transformed House Hightower from wealthy
but relatively minor kings to the greatest

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.459
lords of the Reach.

00:03:01.459 --> 00:03:07.269
Thanks to Lord Lymond's foresight, Highgarden
has always defended Oldtown, allowing the

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:11.569
Hightowers to focus on higher pursuits such
as learning and trade.

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:14.350
Qyburn: On that, we agree.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:20.390
When the Andals invaded Westeros, Lord Dorian
Hightower told his wife of twenty years that

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:26.890
wars are bad for trade, and, to make his point,
traded her for a young Andal princess.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:33.249
When Aegon the Conqueror was earning his name,
House Hightower traded its loyalty to the

00:03:33.249 --> 00:03:41.930
Gardeners for his confirmation of their rights,
welcoming him with open arms and open gates.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:47.510
Pycelle: House Hightower bent the knee to
Aegon to spare the city from his dragons.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:52.400
I would think such insinuations and insults
beneath even you, Qyburn.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:56.590
Then again, mockery of one's betters is the
trade of fools.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.359
Qyburn: I wouldn't know.

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:02.590
Perhaps if I'd studied at the Citadel as long
as you, Grand Maester.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:07.380
Pycelle: The Citadel is the greatest seat
of learning in the known world.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.560
Without it, and the maesters it trains, Westeros
would be a land of superstition and ignorance.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.160
Qyburn: Like the ignorance that shrouds the
Citadel's own birth?

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.911
Pycelle: Even without records, we have more
than enough reason to believe that the Citadel

00:04:21.911 --> 00:04:28.920
began as a court of scholars and priests assembled
by a second son of House Hightower.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.810
When he died, his elder brother raised the
Citadel so that wise men would always have

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:39.170
a home in Westeros and could continue the
inquiries and debates that had given such

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:40.840
joy to his younger brother.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:45.690
Qyburn: His little brother's pets, as legend
has it, he called them.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.110
And so the maesters have remained.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.900
Only their masters have changed.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:57.970
Now they serve every lord with a castle for
a fee, of course, whilst the Citadel serves

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:00.960
its own importance.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:07.270
Pycelle: Every word you speak merely validates
your own expulsion from our ranks.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.140
Qyburn: Knowledge is validation enough.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.690
I assume the world's greatest minds would
agree.

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:16.070
And perhaps they would if I'd met them.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:19.370
Oldtown is a city for old men and old beliefs.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:23.770
Samwell Tarly: The Citadel of Oldtown is the
greatest center of learning in the world,

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.639
not that it has much competition, really.

00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:32.340
Most lords think of books the way they think
of younger sons, useful to have around as

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.960
long as they keep to the corners.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.620
Back when House Hightower still ruled Oldtown
as kings, a crippled younger son sought solace

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:45.930
in books and those who wrote them and became
the greatest patron of learning in his day.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:50.560
Scholars of all sorts flocked to Oldtown to
debate and write and.

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:51.960
debate.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:56.720
After this Prince Peremore's death, his brother
granted land to "Peremore's Pets," as the

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.380
wise men were called, though now they're known
as maesters.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.040
Meera Reed: Mudmen.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:05.820
Bog devils.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:10.710
Those are just the most pleasant names our
fellow Northerners have for us, the crannogmen

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:13.400
who live in the swamps of the Neck.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:19.520
Because we do not live in castles like them.Because
we do not farm like them.Because we are not

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.550
tall or rich like them.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:29.030
But through our veins flows the same blood
of the First Men, and, at times, maybe something

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:30.890
more.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:37.030
We still live much as they did, on floating
islands in houses of thatch and woven reeds.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:42.960
We fish, hunt, and tell our children of our
heroes: the Knight of the Laughing Tree, who

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.580
fought in the Year of the False Spring; the
last Marsh King who challenged the Starks

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:57.010
and lost his crown and his daughter; and other
stories, older still, since lost to the world.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:59.150
The Neck was not always a swamp.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.400
In the Dawn Age, it was as dry and fertile
as the rest of the North.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.960
But during the war with the First Men, the
Children of the Forest brought down the hammer

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.360
of the waters on the Neck, trying to break
Westeros in two.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:18.120
When the waters finally receded, they left
the bogs and swamps we know today.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.870
Many of the First Men decided to fight on,
but my ancestors wisely chose to heed the

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:26.350
Children's power and advance no further.

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:31.600
They beat their swords into frog-spears and
fish-hooks and settled a land forever devastated

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:33.970
by the folly of war.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:40.690
Unlike the rest of Westeros, we keep no garrisons
and raise no soldiers for petty spats with

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:42.220
our neighbors.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.610
Our land protects its own.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:50.900
An outsider will find in the Neck an endless
morass of suckholes, quicksands, and green

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:57.650
grass that looks solid to the unwary eye but
turns to water the instant you treads on it.

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:01.910
If you are lucky enough to be armored, you
will only drown inside your own steel.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:08.800
If you're not, you get to meet what swims
in that water: serpents, and monstrous lizard-lions

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.000
with teeth like daggers and never enough to
eat.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.440
Don't worry.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.310
Only your horse will live long enough to feel
their poisons burning through it.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:24.530
If you somehow survive all this, you may find
that a well-placed dart can be as deadly as

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:29.860
any blade, not that you'll see us blowing
it your way.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:34.310
Since the fall of the last Marsh King, House
Reed has ruled the Neck beneath the banner

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:37.370
of a black lizard-lion on a grey-green field.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:43.260
We are not wealthy, powerful, or known even
to our own countrymen.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:48.340
Our home, Greywater Watch, is no castle you've
ever seen.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:52.410
And seeing it once does not mean you'll ever
find it again.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:56.120
For Greywater Watch moves.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:59.900
Many would-be conquerors have died trying
to find us.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:04.890
With war all around and our Stark lord besieged
on all sides, many more will doubtless soon

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:05.890
try.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:11.050
They will look at us on a map and see a stranglehold
for the North.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:16.650
And they will forget that the sea itself once
entered the Neck and not all of it returned.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:21.870
Jon Snow: Our lands stretches from the Wall
down to the Neck, a narrow land that divides

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:24.690
it from the rest of Westeros.

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:29.360
Legend has it that the Children of the Forest
flooded it in their war against the First

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:30.360
Men.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.800
If that's true, every Northman owes them a
debt of gratitude.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:38.390
The swamps of the Neck are as good as the
Wall for keeping out unwelcome armies.

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:42.110
And if the swamps don't deter you, the crannogmen
should.

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:48.590
Small, shy people who rarely leave those swamps
and who follow House Reed, the gatekeepers

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:54.279
of the North, and among the most important
and loyal bannermen of House Stark.

00:09:54.279 --> 00:09:56.070
Also a bit strange.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:59.380
I heard their oath of fealty once as a child.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.630
It's is like no other lords'.

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:10.150
Ancient and dark, they swear by earth and
water, by bronze and iron, and by ice and

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:10.980
fire.

